  "id": "Compound::DB01595 - Gene::2554",
  "verbalization": "Nitrazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrd and Gabrq, two subunits of the Gaba-a receptor, which are both part of the Gaba-a Receptor Activity. This binding is supported by multiple paths, each with high confidence scores, indicating a consistent interaction between Nitrazepam and the Gaba-a receptor complex. The Gaba-a receptor activity is then linked to Gabra1, the primary subunit of the Gaba-a receptor, suggesting that Nitrazepam modulates the function of Gabra1 through its interaction with the Gaba-a receptor. The evidence supports a direct mechanism by which Nitrazepam influences Gabra1, likely through the activation or modulation of Gaba-a receptor activity. The integration of multiple paths reinforces the notion that Nitrazepam exerts its effects on Gabra1 via a well-defined, biologically plausible mechanism involving the Gaba-a receptor.",
